g('how-to-start-an-import-export-business-in-india', 'money', 'How to start an import export business in India|checklist',
  'How to start an import export business in India: choose a product, register the firm, GST and IEC, find buyers, price, ship and get paid.',
  'You need a firm, a product, a buyer and the right registrations. These are the steps in order.',
  [('Set up', ['Choose a product and a market, and study the demand and the rules.', 'Register a firm: proprietorship, partnership, LLP or company.', 'Get a PAN, a current bank account and GST registration.', 'Apply for the IEC from DGFT.']),
   ('Business side', ['Find buyers or suppliers through trade fairs, B2B sites and export councils.', 'Join an export promotion council and get an RCMC.', 'Work out your price, with a landed cost estimate, and agree the Incoterm and payment terms.']),
   ('Run the shipment', 'Hire a freight forwarder and a customs broker, prepare the documents, ship and collect the payment. Start with small, low risk orders.'),
   ('Protect yourself', 'Use letters of credit or advance payment with new buyers, take cargo insurance, and consider export credit insurance.')],
  [('How much money do I need?', 'It depends on the product. Trading needs working capital for stock and freight.'),
   ('Do I need a licence?', 'Most goods need none. Some need permits, so check the product.'),
   ('How long does it take to get started?', 'Registration takes days to weeks. Finding buyers takes longer.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('ecgc-export-credit-insurance', 'money', 'Export credit insurance|ECGC and how it protects exporters',
  'What export credit insurance is, how ECGC covers non-payment by foreign buyers, and when it is worth it.',
  'If a foreign buyer does not pay, the exporter has the loss. Export credit insurance covers part of it.',
  [('What it covers', 'Commercial risks such as the buyer insolvency or non-payment, and political risks such as war, currency transfer limits and government action. The cover is a percentage of the loss, not all of it.'),
   ('Who offers it', 'ECGC, the government export credit agency of India, and private insurers.'),
   ('How it works', 'You choose a policy, apply for a limit on each buyer, and report the shipments. If the buyer does not pay, you file a claim with the papers.'),
   ('When it pays', 'New buyers, risky countries, large orders and open credit terms. Banks also lend more against insured receivables.')],
  [('Does it cost much?', 'The premium is a small percentage of the insured value, and depends on the buyer and the country.'),
   ('Is it needed for letters of credit?', 'Not usually, but it can cover the issuing bank risk.'),
   ('Can I insure a single shipment?', 'Some policies allow it.')],
  ('contact', 'Talk to our team'))
g('sez-and-eou-explained', 'money', 'SEZ and EOU|special zones and units for exporters',
  'What a Special Economic Zone and an Export Oriented Unit are, the duty benefits, the export obligation and how cargo moves in and out.',
  'SEZs and EOUs let exporters buy inputs and capital goods without duty, in return for exports.',
  [('SEZ', 'A designated area treated as foreign territory for trade. Units import and buy locally without duty and GST, and the goods they make go out as exports. They have a net foreign exchange condition.'),
   ('EOU', 'A unit outside a zone that is approved to export all its production. It gets duty free inputs and capital goods, with bonding and a export obligation.'),
   ('Cargo movement', 'Goods go into an SEZ on a bill of entry for the zone. Sales from an SEZ to the rest of India are treated as imports and pay duty. Goods leaving for export go on a shipping bill.'),
   ('Compliance', 'Both need approvals, bonds, record keeping and reports. Missing the obligation leads to duty and penalty.')],
  [('Is an SEZ unit exempt from GST?', 'Supplies to an SEZ unit are zero rated, under the conditions of the GST rules.'),
   ('Can an EOU sell in India?', 'Only a limited part, with duty, as the policy allows.'),
   ('Where do I apply?', 'With the development commissioner for an SEZ and DGFT for an EOU.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('customs-broker-role-and-licence', 'money', 'Customs broker in India|role, licence and duties',
  'What a customs broker (CHA) does in India, how the licence works under the CBLR 2018, and what a broker owes its client and customs.',
  'A customs broker is licensed by customs to file declarations and deal with the department on behalf of importers and exporters.',
  [('What a broker does', ['Classifies the goods and advises on the duty and the policy.', 'Files bills of entry and shipping bills.', 'Handles examinations, queries and payments, and arranges the release.', 'Keeps records and gives the client the proof of filing.']),
   ('The licence', 'Brokers are licensed under the Customs Brokers Licensing Regulations 2018. The applicant passes an examination and meets the rules on fitness and office. Each licence is tied to a customs area, and the broker and its staff carry identity cards.'),
   ('Duties of a broker', 'Verify the client identity and the IEC and GSTIN, advise the client on compliance, exercise due diligence, not misuse its position and keep records as the rules require.'),
   ('How to check one', 'Ask for the licence number and the customs station, and ask to see the validity. Be careful with unlicensed agents who offer very low prices.')],
  [('Can I file my own bill of entry?', 'Yes, as the importer, but most people use a broker.'),
   ('Is the broker responsible for errors?', 'The importer is responsible for the declaration. A broker may also be liable under the regulations.'),
   ('How are brokers paid?', 'By a fee for each filing and for related services. Ask for the rate list.')],
  ('customs-brokerage', 'See our customs brokerage'))
g('merchanting-trade-explained', 'money', 'Merchanting trade|buying and selling between two other countries',
  'What merchanting trade is: buying goods in one country and selling them to another through an Indian firm, and the RBI rules on it.',
  'In merchanting trade, an Indian firm buys goods from one foreign supplier and sells them to a foreign buyer. The goods never enter India.',
  [('How it works', 'The firm signs a purchase contract and a sale contract, pays the supplier, and collects from the buyer. The goods ship directly between the two countries.'),
   ('RBI conditions', 'The RBI sets conditions, such as the time between payment and receipt, the margin and the documents. The bank checks them under FEMA. Check the current rules.'),
   ('Risks', ['Counterparty risk on both sides.', 'Currency risk between payment and receipt.', 'Sanctions, and the origin of the goods.']),
   ('Paperwork', 'Both contracts, the invoices, the transport documents and the bank records. Our third country trade page explains how we can help.')],
  [('Is the goods movement through Indian customs?', 'No. The goods do not come into India.'),
   ('Do I need an IEC?', 'Yes, for merchanting trade from India.'),
   ('Can profit be taxed?', 'Yes. Take advice from your accountant.')],
  ('third-country-trade', 'See third country trade'))
g('trade-finance-options-for-exporters', 'money', 'Trade finance for exporters|packing credit and bill discounting',
  'The main trade finance tools for exporters in India: pre-shipment packing credit, post-shipment finance, bill discounting and factoring.',
  'Exporters pay for goods before they get paid. Trade finance fills the gap.',
  [('Pre-shipment', 'Packing credit from a bank gives you money to buy raw materials and pack the goods, against an order or a letter of credit. It is repaid from the export proceeds.'),
   ('Post-shipment', 'After shipment, the bank can finance the invoice against the shipping documents, until the buyer pays.'),
   ('Other tools', ['Bill discounting or negotiation under a letter of credit.', 'Factoring and forfaiting for open account sales.', 'Interest equalisation and other support when the scheme is active.']),
   ('What banks ask for', 'Export order, IEC, past records, the buyer details and sometimes credit insurance. Keep the documents in order and ship on time.')],
  [('Is packing credit cheap?', 'It is usually cheaper than a normal working capital loan, and policy support can reduce the rate.'),
   ('How long can I use it?', 'Until the export, within the time that the bank and the RBI rules allow.'),
   ('Do I need a letter of credit?', 'Not always. A firm order may be enough.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('icd-vs-cfs-vs-port-explained', 'local', 'ICD, CFS and port|what is the difference',
  'The difference between an Inland Container Depot, a Container Freight Station and a port in India, and how cargo moves between them.',
  'Indian cargo can be cleared at the port, at a CFS or at an inland depot. Knowing where changes cost and time.',
  [('Port', 'The seaport where ships berth. Customs clears cargo here, and containers are lifted off the ship at the terminal.'),
   ('CFS', 'A container freight station is a warehouse near a port or an ICD that stuffs and destuffs LCL cargo, and may hold containers for examination.'),
   ('ICD', 'An inland container depot is a dry port with a customs office, away from the coast. Containers travel from the port by rail or road, in bond, and are cleared at the ICD.'),
   ('Which is better', 'For cargo to north India, clearing at an ICD near Delhi saves the road leg and lets you clear close to your factory. For urgent cargo, clearing at the port may be faster.')],
  [('Is customs the same at an ICD?', 'Yes. The same bill of entry, and the officers are posted at the ICD.'),
   ('How does cargo travel from port to ICD?', 'In bond, under customs seal, by rail or road.'),
   ('Can LCL go to an ICD?', 'Some ICDs have CFS facilities for LCL. Ask your forwarder.')],
  ('haulage-icd', 'See haulage and ICD services'))
g('delhi-ncr-icds-tughlakabad-dadri-patparganj', 'local', 'Delhi NCR ICDs|Tughlakabad, Patparganj, Dadri, Garhi Harsaru and Loni',
  'The main inland container depots serving Delhi NCR: Tughlakabad, Patparganj, Dadri, Garhi Harsaru and Loni, what each is known for and how to choose.',
  'Delhi has no port, but it has several inland container depots that clear cargo by rail from the western ports.',
  [('The main depots', [('table', ['ICD', 'Location', 'Known for'], [['Tughlakabad (TKD)', 'South Delhi', 'Large rail served ICD, many importers and exporters'], ['Patparganj', 'East Delhi', 'Close to east Delhi and Ghaziabad industry'], ['Dadri', 'Greater Noida, UP', 'End of the western freight corridor'], ['Garhi Harsaru', 'Gurugram, Haryana', 'Serves Gurugram and Manesar'], ['Loni', 'Ghaziabad, UP', 'Serves the Ghaziabad industrial belt']], 'Services and terminal operators change. Confirm with your forwarder.')]),
   ('How to choose', 'Pick the depot nearest to the factory or warehouse, with the shipping line rail service you need and the free days you get. The road distance to the plant matters, as does the line cut-off.'),
   ('Documents', 'The same as for a port. The importer files the bill of entry for the ICD, and the exporter files the shipping bill with the ICD as the place of stuffing.'),
   ('Air cargo', 'Delhi air cargo at IGI airport has its own customs and terminals for air freight.')],
  [('Is Dadri better than Tughlakabad?', 'It depends on where your plant is and the rail service from your port.'),
   ('Can I clear at the port instead?', 'Yes, and then truck the cargo. That is longer by road and costs more.'),
   ('How long from Mundra or JNPT to Delhi?', 'By rail, a few days. The freight corridor has cut it.')],
  ('haulage-icd', 'See haulage and ICD services'))
g('jnpt-nhava-sheva-port-guide', 'local', 'JNPT (Nhava Sheva)|India\'s biggest container port',
  'A guide to JNPT at Nhava Sheva near Mumbai: terminals, what it handles, how cargo moves out and what importers and exporters should know.',
  'Jawaharlal Nehru Port, known as JNPT or Nhava Sheva, is India largest container port. It sits across the harbour from Mumbai, in Navi Mumbai.',
  [('What it handles', 'Containers for most of western and northern India, from textiles and engineering goods to chemicals and food. It has several container terminals run by large global operators.'),
   ('Moving cargo', 'Cargo goes out by road to Mumbai and Pune and by rail to the northern ICDs, including the Delhi depots. The western freight corridor links it to the north.'),
   ('Codes', 'The customs port code is INNSA1.'),
   ('Tips', ['Check the terminal for your container, because rules and free days can differ.', 'Pre-file the bill of entry, and plan the truck slot.', 'Watch the traffic on the approach roads in the peak season.'])],
  [('Is JNPT the same as Nhava Sheva?', 'Yes. Both names are used for the same port.'),
   ('Which is better for Delhi, JNPT or Mundra?', 'See our Mundra vs JNPT guide.'),
   ('How do I track a container at JNPT?', 'Use the terminal portal or our tracking hub.')],
  ('port-airport-codes', 'Open the port and airport codes'))
g('mundra-port-guide', 'local', 'Mundra port|India\'s largest private port',
  'A guide to Mundra port in Gujarat: what it handles, rail links to north India, the SEZ, and what importers and exporters should know.',
  'Mundra, in Kutch, Gujarat, is India largest private port. It has deep water, large container terminals and strong rail links to the north.',
  [('What it handles', 'Containers, bulk, liquids and vehicles. It is a major gateway for north and west India and for cargo with big shipping lines.'),
   ('Rail and road', 'Double stack container trains run from Mundra to the Delhi region. Trucks serve Gujarat and Rajasthan.'),
   ('Codes', 'The customs port code is INMUN1.'),
   ('Tips', ['Compare the line rail and ICD options for your destination.', 'Check free days and charges at the terminal.', 'Pre-file the bill of entry for faster release.'])],
  [('Is Mundra cheaper than JNPT?', 'It depends on the line, the service and the inland leg. Compare the full door to door cost.'),
   ('Does YAM operate at Mundra?', 'Yes. We have a port office there.'),
   ('Is Mundra an SEZ?', 'Part of the port area is in an SEZ.')],
  ('port-airport-codes', 'Open the port and airport codes'))
g('mundra-vs-jnpt-which-port', 'local', 'Mundra vs JNPT|which port for Delhi and north India',
  'Mundra or JNPT? How to compare the two big ports for cargo to or from Delhi and north India: rail, free days, lines and total cost.',
  'Both ports serve Delhi by rail. The best choice depends on the line, the service and the whole route, not the port alone.',
  [('What to compare', [('table', ['Point', 'What to check'], [['Shipping line and service', 'Which lines call, how often, and the transit time'], ['Free days', 'At the port and at the ICD'], ['Rail', 'Which ICD and how many days by train'], ['Charges', 'Terminal, rail and trucking, door to door'], ['Reliability', 'Past delays, the congestion and the rolling of boxes']])]),
   ('General pattern', 'Mundra often has fast rail links to the north. JNPT is nearer to Mumbai and Pune, and has more services on some trades. The best port for you can differ by trade lane.'),
   ('How to decide', 'Ask your forwarder for a door to door quote from each port, with the same free days. Compare the total cost and the transit time.'),
   ('Keep options open', 'Choose a forwarder that can use both ports, so you can switch when one is congested.')],
  [('Is one always better?', 'No. It changes with the line, the season and the rail schedule.'),
   ('What about air cargo?', 'Air goes through Delhi, Mumbai and other airports. The sea port choice does not apply.'),
   ('Can I ask for both quotes?', 'Yes. We give door to door quotes from either port.')],
  ('india-transit-times', 'See India transit times'))
g('dedicated-freight-corridor-containers', 'local', 'Dedicated Freight Corridor|what it means for container cargo',
  'How the Dedicated Freight Corridor helps container cargo between the western ports and north India, and what exporters and importers gain.',
  'The Dedicated Freight Corridors are freight-only rail lines. The western one links the Mumbai area ports with the Delhi region.',
  [('What it is', 'A rail line made for goods trains, separate from passenger traffic. It allows longer, heavier trains and double stack container trains.'),
   ('Why it matters', ['Faster and more reliable rail transit between the ports and Delhi NCR.', 'Lower cost per container, as trains carry more.', 'Less pressure on the roads.']),
   ('Where it ends', 'The western corridor runs from the Mumbai area to Dadri in the Delhi region. The eastern one runs from Punjab to West Bengal. Check which ICDs connect to the corridor.'),
   ('What to ask', 'Ask your forwarder or line whether the service uses the corridor, and how many days to your ICD.')],
  [('Does it help LCL?', 'Indirectly, through the faster container flow.'),
   ('Are all ICDs on the corridor?', 'No. Some are reached by feeder lines.'),
   ('Does it change customs?', 'No. The customs process is the same.')],
  ('india-transit-times', 'See India transit times'))
g('un-locode-and-customs-port-codes-india', 'local', 'Port codes in India|UN/LOCODE and customs location codes',
  'How port and location codes work in India: UN/LOCODE, ICEGATE location codes, and examples such as INNSA1, INMUN1 and INDEL4.',
  'Every port, ICD and airport has a code. They are on the bill of entry, the shipping bill and the manifest, and a wrong one causes problems.',
  [('UN/LOCODE', 'Five characters: two for the country and three for the place. India starts with IN, for example INNSA for Nhava Sheva.'),
   ('Customs location code', 'Indian customs adds a digit to the code. For example INNSA1 is JNPT, INMUN1 is Mundra, INTKD6 is ICD Tughlakabad and INDEL4 is Delhi air cargo.'),
   ('Where you use them', 'In the bill of entry, shipping bill, EGM and IGM, and on ICEGATE. The digit shows the type of location, such as a sea port or an ICD.'),
   ('Find a code', 'Our port and airport codes tool lets you search by name.')],
  [('What is the last digit?', 'It is the customs category of the location, such as sea, ICD, air or land.'),
   ('Is the airport code the same as the IATA code?', 'The IATA code has three letters, such as DEL. The customs code adds IN and a digit.'),
   ('Where do I check a code?', 'On ICEGATE or in our tool.')],
  ('port-airport-codes', 'Open the port and airport codes'))
g('indian-ports-overview', 'local', 'Indian ports|an overview of the main container gateways',
  'The main Indian seaports for containers, JNPT, Mundra, Chennai, Visakhapatnam, Kolkata, Cochin and others, and what each serves.',
  'India has a dozen big ports on a long coast. Each serves its own hinterland.',
  [('West coast', ['JNPT (Nhava Sheva) and Mundra: the biggest, serving the north and west.', 'Pipavav, Hazira and Deendayal (Kandla): Gujarat gateways.', 'Cochin, with the Vallarpadam terminal: the south west.']),
   ('East and south', ['Chennai and Kamarajar (Ennore): the south and Tamil Nadu.', 'Tuticorin (V.O. Chidambaranar): the deep south.', 'Visakhapatnam and Krishnapatnam: Andhra Pradesh.', 'Kolkata and Haldia: the east and Nepal trade.']),
   ('How to choose', 'Pick the port that is closest to your cargo, with the services you need. For the north, JNPT and Mundra lead because of rail. For the south, Chennai and Tuticorin.'),
   ('Coastal shipping', 'Coastal shipping between Indian ports is growing, and it can be a cheap option for heavy cargo.')],
  [('Which is the biggest port?', 'JNPT for containers in the public and private mix, and Mundra for the largest private port.'),
   ('Which port for Nepal?', 'Kolkata or Visakhapatnam, by rail.'),
   ('Where do I find transit times?', 'Our India transit times page lists estimates.')],
  ('india-transit-times', 'See India transit times'))
